=[tree-sitter-cotomet]

==[tree-sitter-cotomet]

Rust binding for the cotomet tree-sitter grammar (`grammar.js` at the crate
root; `src/parser.c` etc. are generated from it by
`npx tree-sitter-cli@0.26.12 generate`, then compiled by `build.rs`).

This grammar exists for editor syntax highlighting of a standalone cotomet
file (`.ctmt`), not as a source of truth -- that is `cotomet-syntax`'s job. It
is an approximation, and `tests/src/cotomet.rs` in the `tomet-tests` package
runs a corpus through both to catch the two drifting apart. What it
simplifies:

- @strong[A `:` ends a key only at the start of an entry.] The parser reads
`note: see: this` as the text `see: this`, and so does the grammar; but
in a file or a `{...}` an entry that starts `x: y` is a pair, and a bare
scalar there holds a `:` only when text runs straight on after it
(`12:30`). Entries need no separator in the grammar, where the parser
wants a newline or a `,` (it reads the rest of the line as one scalar).
- @strong[A bare scalar is not typed here.] `null`, `true`, `false` and a
number are the highlight query's business, decided from the node's
text, so the tree has one `scalar` node for all of them.
- @strong[Call arguments are values only.] `list(a: 1)` is an error here as it
is for the parser, but `list(a: b)` reads as the scalar `a: b`, which
the parser also does.
- @strong[A top-level bare value is accepted.] A `.ctmt` file is `key: value`
entries only (`cotomet_syntax::parse_file`); the grammar reads a stray
value as the value it is.
- @strong[A declaration may stand anywhere an entry may.] The parser reads
`::name(args){data}` only before the first entry; the grammar has no
notion of "before", so one after an entry is read as a declaration too.
- @strong[No block comments.] cotomet has `//` only; `/* */` is a tomet
document construct.

==[Constants]

===[const LANGUAGE]

The tree-sitter language function for cotomet. Convert to a
`tree_sitter::Language` via `.into()` to build a `tree_sitter::Parser`.

===[const NODE_TYPES]

The grammar's `node-types.json`.

===[const HIGHLIGHTS_QUERY]

The grammar's `highlights.scm` query string for syntax highlighting.
